&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;What follows is a composite incident. The shape is real and it has played out at multiple Go shops; there's a &lt;a href="https://skoredin.pro/blog/golang/goroutine-leak-debugging" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;public writeup of essentially the same outage&lt;/a&gt;, and Uber's research team published &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12002" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;a paper&lt;/a&gt; on how this exact pattern accumulates across large Go codebases. Names, numbers, and timestamps below are illustrative.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A notification service pushes alerts to half a million connected clients. Sunday around three in the morning, the box hits 47GB of resident memory on hardware provisioned for 16. p99 on the websocket fanout climbs past thirty seconds. Heartbeats time out. Dashboard goes red and the on-call gets paged.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First move is the usual one: SSH into the box, hit pprof, count goroutines.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight console"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gp"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; localhost:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;head&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="go"&gt;goroutine profile: total 50318
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Fifty thousand goroutines on a service that should idle around two hundred. The Grafana count has been climbing in an almost-straight line for six weeks, which means whatever broke shipped six weeks ago and nobody noticed because the box had headroom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A restart drops memory to 400MB and pulls p99 back to 80ms. The pager quiets down. The on-call writes "investigating root cause" in the incident channel and goes back to bed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bug, found the next morning, is one line. A &lt;code&gt;cancel&lt;/code&gt; function returned by &lt;code&gt;context.WithCancel&lt;/code&gt;, captured in a struct field, never called when the websocket disconnected. Six weeks of accumulation, hidden behind enough RAM until peak Sunday traffic pushed the box over the cliff.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The One Line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The buggy file looks unremarkable. It's a notification subscription, the kind of thing every Go service that fans messages out to clients ends up writing.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Subscription&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;ID&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Events&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;chan&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Event&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;CancelFunc&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NotificationService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;parent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;userID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Subscription&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WithCancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Subscription&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="n"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;Events&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;make&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;chan&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;16&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;go&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pumpEvents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;userID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Events&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mu&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Lock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;subs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mu&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Unlock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The websocket handler that owns the connection does this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WSHandler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Serve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;upgrade&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;notif&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;userIDFrom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Events&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;writeJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The handler looks correct on a careful read. The connection upgrades, the subscription opens, events stream out until the write fails, and then &lt;code&gt;defer conn.Close()&lt;/code&gt; runs and the function returns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What it does not do is call &lt;code&gt;sub.cancel()&lt;/code&gt;. The &lt;code&gt;cancel&lt;/code&gt; function lives in the subscription struct and the caller never invokes it. The &lt;code&gt;pumpEvents&lt;/code&gt; goroutine is parked on &lt;code&gt;ctx.Done()&lt;/code&gt; waiting for a signal that will never arrive. The &lt;code&gt;Subscription&lt;/code&gt; struct is still in &lt;code&gt;s.subs&lt;/code&gt; because nobody removed it, which means the channel it owns is still reachable. Even Go 1.26's experimental &lt;a href="https://go.dev/doc/diagnostics" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;goroutine leak profile&lt;/a&gt; cannot detect it: the goroutine looks live to the GC, because the channel is rooted in a service-level map.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the worst kind of leak. Neither the runtime, the compiler, nor &lt;code&gt;go vet&lt;/code&gt; can see it. &lt;code&gt;runtime.NumGoroutine()&lt;/code&gt; ticks up by one every time a websocket disconnects. Multiply by the connection rate of a real product and six weeks gets you fifty thousand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix, in the handler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WSHandler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Serve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;upgrade&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;notif&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;userIDFrom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;notif&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Unsubscribe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c"&gt;// calls sub.cancel()&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Events&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;writeJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One line. &lt;code&gt;defer h.notif.Unsubscribe(sub.ID)&lt;/code&gt;, immediately after &lt;code&gt;Subscribe&lt;/code&gt;. The implementation of &lt;code&gt;Unsubscribe&lt;/code&gt; calls &lt;code&gt;cancel()&lt;/code&gt;, removes the subscription from the map, and closes the channel. The &lt;code&gt;pumpEvents&lt;/code&gt; goroutine sees &lt;code&gt;ctx.Done()&lt;/code&gt;, exits, and the leak stops growing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the entire production fix. Everything that follows is how you stop writing this bug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why It Hid for Six Weeks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Goroutines are cheap. The runtime allocates 2KB per stack and grows it as needed. Fifty thousand parked at 2KB each is 100MB. That alone would not page anyone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What pages people is what those goroutines hold. Each &lt;code&gt;pumpEvents&lt;/code&gt; instance kept a buffered channel, a subscriber registration with the upstream message bus, captured maps, and a slice of user state. Fifty thousand closures retaining a few hundred KB each is how 47GB happens.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pipeline kept publishing into the leaked channels. Buffer fills, GC ticks more often, p99 climbs, but the service still serves traffic. No error log. The metric that catches this is &lt;code&gt;go_goroutines&lt;/code&gt; from the Prometheus exporter. If the line is not flat or sawtooth-shaped, you have a leak. Most teams don't put it on their on-call dashboard until after their first 3 AM page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Habit 1: Defer the Cancel on the Same Line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every &lt;code&gt;context.WithCancel&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;context.WithTimeout&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;context.WithDeadline&lt;/code&gt; returns a cancel function the caller must invoke. The &lt;a href="https://pkg.go.dev/context" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;context package documentation&lt;/a&gt; says it directly: "Failing to call the CancelFunc leaks the child and its children until the parent is canceled."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The habit that prevents this is mechanical. The line that creates the context and the line that defers the cancel are the same edit, every time:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WithCancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the context has to outlive the function (this was the case in the bug above), so a plain &lt;code&gt;defer cancel()&lt;/code&gt; won't work. The owner of the context then has to arrange for cancel to fire on the lifecycle event that ends the work. For a websocket subscription, that's the connection close. For a worker pool, the pool shutdown. For a request-scoped fan-out, request completion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The smell to watch for in review: a &lt;code&gt;cancel&lt;/code&gt; function captured into a struct field with no obvious place where it gets called. Every time you see one, ask "what triggers this cancel and is that trigger guaranteed to fire?" If the answer is "the caller is supposed to," wire a defer at the caller and stop relying on discipline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;go vet&lt;/code&gt; runs a &lt;a href="https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/vet" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;lostcancel&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; check that catches the in-function variant. It will not catch the cross-function pattern that bit production at 3 AM. The compiler is not your friend here. The habit is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Habit 2: Cancel on Every Spawn
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mental model that makes this stop happening: every &lt;code&gt;go func&lt;/code&gt; you write should answer the question "what makes this goroutine exit." A goroutine exits when its function returns, when a channel it reads from gets closed, or when a context it's watching gets cancelled. That's the list. The runtime will guarantee any of the three. It will not guarantee "the request will finish," "it won't run long enough to matter," or "someone else will stop sending." Wishes don't compile.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the goroutine outlives the function that spawned it, the context is almost always the right exit signal. That's what &lt;code&gt;pumpEvents&lt;/code&gt; was supposed to use. It read &lt;code&gt;ctx.Done()&lt;/code&gt; correctly. The bug was upstream: nobody fired the cancel. The worker itself did its job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every spawn site should have a context derived from the lifecycle of the work. A request-scoped goroutine derives from &lt;code&gt;r.Context()&lt;/code&gt;. A connection-scoped goroutine derives from a context that ends with the connection. A service-scoped worker derives from the service's root context:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;work&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Done&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two exit conditions. Channel close, context cancel. No worker outlives the service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Habit 3: Derive Contexts, Don't Detach Them
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other shape of this bug is a goroutine that should have been tied to its caller and got detached:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;go&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;audit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c"&gt;// wrong: no context, no link to caller&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;audit&lt;/code&gt; goroutine has nothing to listen on. The worse mutation hard-codes &lt;code&gt;context.Background()&lt;/code&gt; because the developer wants the work to outlive the request. That's sometimes correct. Most of the time the goroutine pays for it by having no shutdown signal at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The right pattern derives from a longer-lived context, not from &lt;code&gt;Background()&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCtx&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;CancelFunc&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCtx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WithCancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCtx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCtx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;go&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;audit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCtx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c"&gt;// outlives request, dies with service&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Shutdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bgCancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The audit goroutine outlives the request but not the service. Shutdown is honest. A goroutine without a context that can cancel it has decided to leak under sufficiently bad conditions. Whether it owns one should be a deliberate choice rather than an accident of which parameters got passed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Habit 4: Make goleak the CI Gate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The highest-ROI tooling change for any Go codebase is one file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mypkg&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"testing"&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"go.uber.org/goleak"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TestMain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;testing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;M&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;goleak&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;VerifyTestMain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;go.uber.org/goleak&lt;/code&gt; snapshots the goroutine list at the start and end of the test run. If anything is left over (and the leftover isn't on the ignore list), the suite fails with stack traces pointing at the leaked goroutine. The &lt;a href="https://github.com/uber-go/goleak" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;goleak README&lt;/a&gt; documents the API; one TestMain per package and you are wired in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first run will fail in places you did not expect. The race detector starts a goroutine. Some HTTP test servers leave one running. &lt;code&gt;goleak.IgnoreCurrent()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;goleak.IgnoreTopFunction("name")&lt;/code&gt; exist for known-safe leftovers; use them sparingly with a comment. If you silence everything until the suite turns green, you've hidden real leaks under test noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;goleak only catches what your tests exercise. The production bug above would not have shown up in unit tests, because the unit test for &lt;code&gt;Subscribe&lt;/code&gt; calls &lt;code&gt;Unsubscribe&lt;/code&gt; correctly. The integration test probably never covered disconnect-mid-stream. goleak in CI is the floor; production telemetry is the ceiling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the ceiling, you need production data. Uber published &lt;a href="https://www.uber.com/blog/leakprof-featherlight-in-production-goroutine-leak-detection/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LeakProf&lt;/a&gt;, an in-production detector that pulls goroutine profiles from running services and aggregates parked goroutines by source location. Stacks that show up thousands of times at the same line are leaks, almost by definition. The blog post walks through fixes that reduced peak memory by 2.5x and 5x in two specific services. The follow-up &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12002" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv paper&lt;/a&gt; reports LeakProf finding 24 goroutine leaks across the monorepo (21 fixed) and up to 9.2x memory reduction in some services. The same approach is buildable on top of &lt;code&gt;pprof.Lookup("goroutine")&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Go 1.26 ships an experimental &lt;code&gt;GOEXPERIMENT=goroutineleakprofile&lt;/code&gt; (the &lt;a href="https://tip.golang.org/doc/go1.26" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;release notes&lt;/a&gt; describe the new &lt;code&gt;goroutineleak&lt;/code&gt; profile and the &lt;code&gt;/debug/pprof/goroutineleak&lt;/code&gt; endpoint). It's the runtime-native version, using GC reachability, and the team intends to make it default in 1.27. It catches the channel-orphan flavor. It will not catch the scenario above, because the leaked subscription stayed reachable through the service map. Both detection modes have to coexist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reading the Profile
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the page fires, the dump is what you want. &lt;code&gt;pprof.Lookup("goroutine").WriteTo(w, 2)&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;debug=2&lt;/code&gt; produces stack traces in the same format Go uses on panic. One stack per goroutine, with the wait state and duration:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;goroutine 48273 [chan receive, 18 minutes]:
github.com/example/notif.(*Service).pumpEvents(...)
    /app/notif/service.go:142 +0xa5
created by github.com/example/notif.(*Service).Subscribe
    /app/notif/service.go:88 +0x1c4
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The wait state is the diagnosis. &lt;code&gt;[chan receive]&lt;/code&gt; is parked on a read. &lt;code&gt;[chan send]&lt;/code&gt; is parked on a write. &lt;code&gt;[select]&lt;/code&gt; is a select with no clause ready. &lt;code&gt;[semacquire]&lt;/code&gt; is a &lt;code&gt;WaitGroup.Wait&lt;/code&gt; or mutex acquire that has not completed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trick on a real dump with thousands of stacks is grouping. Sort and uniq the function lines:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;awk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/^goroutine/{flag=1; next} flag &amp;amp;&amp;amp; /^$/{flag=0} flag'&lt;/span&gt; dump.txt &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
    | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;uniq&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rn&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;head&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On the leaked service above, the top of that output looks roughly like this (illustrative, not a captured run):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;49872 github.com/example/notif.(*Service).pumpEvents
   ...
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Forty-nine thousand stacks at the same source line. That's the leak. The remaining few hundred goroutines are doing real work. A goroutine parked for eighteen minutes when the work that spawned it should have finished in seconds is also a leak, even if there's only one of it. Add a duration filter to your dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Uber paper &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12002" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unveiling and Vanquishing Goroutine Leaks in Enterprise Microservices&lt;/a&gt; studied 75 million lines of Go across 2,500 microservices. goleak alone caught 857 pre-existing leaks in legacy code and prevented around 260 new leaks over a year. LeakProf found another 24 in services that had passed CI. The order matters: catch the pattern in CI, catch the runtime accumulation in production, read the profile when something escapes both. None of the three replaces the others.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The one-line bug at the top of this post passed CI. It would have passed any review that did not ask "what triggers this cancel, and is that trigger guaranteed to fire?" That question is the whole post compressed into twelve words. Apply it to every &lt;code&gt;context.WithCancel&lt;/code&gt;, every &lt;code&gt;go func&lt;/code&gt;, every channel you create.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to Do Tomorrow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pick the cheapest of the moves below that your team hasn't already shipped. Then come back next sprint and do the next one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;go_goroutines&lt;/code&gt; to your service dashboard. If you cannot see the count, you cannot see the leak. The Prometheus exporter ships it for free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;goleak.VerifyTestMain(m)&lt;/code&gt; to your most concurrent package: workers, queues, or websocket handlers, whichever one scares you most. Watch the test suite turn red. Fix the leaks one at a time. Then add the next package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read the next &lt;code&gt;go func&lt;/code&gt; in your PR queue with the three-answer rule in hand. If the answer to "what makes this exit" isn't &lt;em&gt;function returns&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;channel closes&lt;/em&gt;, or &lt;em&gt;context cancels&lt;/em&gt;, send the PR back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When &lt;code&gt;GOEXPERIMENT=goroutineleakprofile&lt;/code&gt; becomes default in 1.27, that endpoint becomes free signal. Wire your dashboard now so the day it lands you already know what flat looks like.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  If this was useful
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Goroutines, channels, and context cancellation are where most production Go bugs live. Most tutorials brush past them in a paragraph. &lt;em&gt;The Complete Guide to Go Programming&lt;/em&gt; walks the language end to end at the level the runtime actually demands: the memory model, the scheduler, channel semantics, context propagation, and the patterns that prevent the bug above from being written in the first place. This post covers about three pages of one chapter. The book covers the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

